markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Catching Problems

#### We’ve all seen screens like these. Whether it’s a little dinosaur game when the Wi-Fi drops or a **404** message on a website, these screens are a developer’s way of handling a problem.  



---

## Compare the the examples

#### Let's consider the **user experience** when a system failure occurs.

<!-- Replace these placeholder images with your actual error screenshots -->
<div style="display: flex; gap: 10px; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 15px 0;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/74672c08f38c7654a1da61fd28d86e30-image-1772645041745.png" alt="Error screen 1" style="max-width: 30%; min-width: 150px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 8px;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/493934177809094b22f8745881be9500-image-1772645102652.png" alt="Error screen 2" style="max-width: 30%; min-width: 150px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 8px;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/c7667ed421e814c42f15cfd90f4f8ab6-image-1772645150534.png" alt="Error screen 3" style="max-width: 30%; min-width: 150px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 8px;">
</div>

---

## 💬 Reflect

#### Look at each of the images and reflect on the prompts:

#### 1. **How helpful is the error message?**  
   -  #### Does it explain what went wrong?  
   - #### Does it suggest what the user can do next?

#### 2. **How did the page make you feel?**  
   - #### Calm and reassured?  
   - #### Confused or stuck?  
   - #### Blamed or at fault?

#### 3. **Rank the failure screens from least to most trustworthy.**  
   - #### **1** = least trustworthy  
   - #### **3** = most trustworthy  


---

#### ✅ Check your thinking

<div style="background-color: #E6E6E6; border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px; font-size: 14px; margin-top: 10px;">

<details>
  <summary style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>Open to see one possible analysis</b></summary>
  <p style=" font-size: 14px; margin-top: 10px; line-height: 1.5;">
    As you look across the three screens, the key idea is that <b>how a system fails matters as much as whether it fails</b>.
  </p>
  <ul style="line-height: 1.5; padding-left: 20px;">
    <li style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>A friendly fallback</b> (clear message, calm tone, maybe a simple illustration or small game) communicates <b>planning and care</b>. It feels like the developers expected problems and prepared for them.</li>
    <li style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>A blank or nearly blank screen</b> (little text, no guidance) can communicate <b>neglect or instability</b>. Users may worry that the site is broken or unsafe.</li>
    <li style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>Exposed technical errors</b> (stack traces, code, or database messages) can signal a lack of <b>user-centered design</b>. The system is talking to other developers, not to the user.</li>
    </ul>
  <p style="line-height: 1.5; font-size: 14px;">
    As you compare and rank the screens, you should begin to see <b>trust</b> as a product of intentional error handling. A well-designed error screen supports the user, even when things go wrong.
  </p>
  <p style="line-height: 1.5; font-size: 14px;">
    There is no single "correct" ranking, but a common pattern might be:
  </p>
  <ol style="line-height: 1.5; padding-left: 20px;">
    <li style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>Rank 3 (most trustworthy)</b>: Screen with a friendly design, clear explanation, and next steps.</li>
    <li style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>Rank 2</b>: Screen with a simple error message and maybe a link back, but little extra support.</li>
    <li style= "font-size: 14px;"><b>Rank 1 (least trustworthy)</b>: Screen that is blank, overly technical, or makes the user feel at fault.</li>
  </ol>
</details>

</div>

---

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 40px; color: #000000; width: 95%; padding: 1px 20px 1px 20px; margin-top: 20px;">

<p style="line-height: 1.5; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 18px; ">
<b> 💡Teacher Tip: </b>Invite students to talk about the <b>"vibe"</b> of each page, not just the words. Is it playful and apologetic, or cold and technical? Ask questions like, “Which screen would you trust with your credit card?” or “Which one feels like the team cared about you as a user?” This language sets up the idea of <b>Human-Centered Reliability</b>—designing systems so that even failures feel predictable, respectful, and safe.
</p>
</div>


MARKDOWN
